{% from "macros.njk" import callout, snippet with context %}

{% set design %}
<section class="docs-section">
  <h2 id="d-usage">Usage</h2>
  <p>The top app bar displays information and actions relating to the current
     screen: branding, screen titles, navigation and actions. It sits at the
     top of every screen in the app and can transform into a
     <a href="#d-contextual">contextual action bar</a> when items are
     selected.</p>
  <p>In Flint it is part of the
     <a href="bottom-nav.html">shell</a>: the first grid row of
     <code>fl-shell</code>, above the scrolling content. Below 600px it is
     56px tall (M2's mobile spec); above, 64px.</p>
  {{ callout({
    variant: "info",
    title: "Mobile-first, so some M2 variations are not here",
    text: "Extended, prominent and dense bars, imagery in bars and iOS translucency are permanently discarded — native-feel apps on phones don't use them. The regular bar plus the contextual action bar cover what those apps actually do. See “Deliberately not in Flint” below.",
    href: "#d-not-in-flint",
    linkText: "The discard list"
  }) }}
</section>

<section class="docs-section">
  <h2 id="d-anatomy">Anatomy</h2>
  <p>M2's recommended left-to-right placement, which the flex row gives you
     by just writing the elements in order:</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Navigation icon</strong> (optional) — a menu icon opening a
        drawer, an up arrow, or a back arrow; an
        <code>fl-icon-btn</code> at the far left.</li>
    <li><strong>Title</strong> (optional) — <code>fl-top-bar__title</code>:
        the screen, the section or the app. It never wraps — long titles
        truncate. Left-aligned is the web/desktop idiom (M2's
        cross-platform note: iOS centers by default; that is a platform
        convention, not something Flint ships).</li>
    <li><strong>Action items</strong> (optional) — <code>fl-icon-btn</code>s,
        most-used on the left, progressing to the least-used.</li>
    <li><strong>Overflow menu</strong> (optional) — the ⋮ at the far right;
        anything that doesn't fit goes here. It is a real
        <a href="menu.html">menu</a>.</li>
  </ol>
</section>

<section class="docs-section">
  <h2 id="d-actions">Actions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Order by frequency:</strong> most-used action first
        (leftmost), overflow last (M2's icon-placement rule). Actions move
        into the overflow as the bar narrows — curate by hand, don't
        auto-collapse.</li>
    <li><strong>Icon-only actions need an aria-label</strong> ("Search", not
        the bare icon). The ⋮ is "More options".</li>
    <li><strong>Badges</strong> (unread counts) sit on the icon button — see
        the <a href="bottom-nav.html">shell demo</a>.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="docs-section">
  <h2 id="d-transparent">Transparent bar</h2>
  <p>Google's actual web apps (Keep, Drive) don't draw the bar as a filled
     strip: it sits on the page surface with no fill and no border, and the
     controls — the big rounded search pill — are the visual anchor.
     <code>fl-top-bar--transparent</code> is that pattern:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>What changes:</strong> the fill and the 1px border go
        transparent (private tokens — two lines of CSS). Height, safe-area
        inset, layout and focus behavior are unchanged.</li>
    <li><strong>Deviation, recorded:</strong> M2 gives the top app bar its
        own surface. Flint follows Google's web apps instead — when
        everything is one surface, a bar border would fake separation the
        content doesn't have. This is <em>not</em> iOS translucency: nothing
        scrolls under the bar and nothing is blurred.</li>
    <li><strong>Search in the bar:</strong> the pill is a real
        <code>fl-field</code> wrapped in <code>fl-top-bar__search</code> —
        label-less input with an <code>aria-label</code>, 48px tall (M2's
        search bar height, via the scoped <code>--fl-field-h</code> token),
        fully rounded. The pill's own border is what anchors a transparent
        bar; without it the bar reads as a floating toolbar.</li>
    <li><strong>Contextual action bar still fills:</strong> even on a
        transparent bar, <code>fl-top-bar--contextual</code> takes the
        inverse-surface fill — a state change you can't see isn't a state
        change. The transparent variant only mutes the resting state.</li>
    <li><strong>Scroll-hide:</strong> works unchanged, and arguably looks
        better — a borderless bar slides away and leaves nothing behind.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="docs-section">
  <h2 id="d-scrolling">Scrolling</h2>
  <p>M2: scrolling upward hides the bar; scrolling downward reveals it. Add
     <code>data-fl-scroll-hide</code> to the bar and flint.js watches the
     shell's <code>fl-content</code> pane (or the window, for bars outside a
     shell), toggling <code>is-hidden</code> past a 4px threshold:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>The bar slides up and the shell's bar row collapses — the content
        reclaims the space (no dead stripe left behind).</li>
    <li>Scrolling up at any point reveals the bar; at the very top it always
        shows, even after a downward fling.</li>
    <li><code>prefers-reduced-motion</code> removes the slide.</li>
    <li><strong>No-JS fallback (honest):</strong> without flint.js the bar
        simply stays put — nothing hides, nothing breaks.</li>
  </ul>
  <p><em>Deviation, recorded:</em> M2 grows the bar's elevation when content
     scrolls behind it. Flint has no shadows, and the bar's 1px bottom
     border is already the constant separation — so there is nothing to
     switch on scroll.</p>
  <p><strong>Safe area:</strong> the bar pads itself with
     <code>env(safe-area-inset-top)</code>, so an installed PWA under a
     status bar or a notch keeps its controls below it. Pair with
     <code>viewport-fit=cover</code> on the meta viewport. In a browser the
     inset is 0 and nothing changes.</p>
</section>

<section class="docs-section">
  <h2 id="d-contextual">Contextual action bar</h2>
  <p>When the user selects items (photos, messages, notes), the bar
     transforms: the fill recolors, the navigation icon becomes a close
     icon, the title reads "N selected", and the actions become the
     contextual ones. On close it transforms back.</p>
  <p>Flint ships the state, not the state machine:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>fl-top-bar--contextual</code> recolors the bar to the
        <em>inverse surface</em> — the same mapping as the
        <a href="snackbar.html">snackbar</a> (<code>--fl-text</code> fill,
        <code>--fl-surface</code> text, light state layers on the dark
        fill).</li>
    <li>The bar holds two <code>fl-top-bar__group</code> slot groups — the
        default one and the contextual one; exactly one is
        <code>[hidden]</code>. <code>display: contents</code> keeps the
        bar's flex as the single layout owner.</li>
    <li>The swap is author logic (selection is app state — the same
        contract as the menu's <code>aria-checked</code>): one class and
        two <code>hidden</code> flips. The demo's script is four
        statements.</li>
    <li>The contextual group stays in the DOM, so the swap works with JS
        off — whatever toggles it (a form control, a link, a checkbox) is
        author code.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="docs-section">
  <h2 id="d-accessibility">Accessibility</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The bar is a <code>&lt;header&gt;</code>; the title is a real
        heading (h1/h2 per document outline).</li>
    <li>Every icon-only action carries a specific
        <code>aria-label</code>; "Notifications, 3 unread" beats a bare
        bell.</li>
    <li>The overflow trigger uses <code>aria-haspopup="menu"</code> +
        <code>aria-expanded</code> — the
        <a href="menu.html">menu</a> contract.</li>
    <li>Scroll-hide never removes the bar from the accessibility tree or
        the tab order — it is translated off-screen, so keyboard users
        still reach its actions; they are never trapped by scroll
        position.</li>
    <li>Contextual bar: the close button's label is "Close selection", and
        the count text is a plain text node screen readers read as the
        title.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="docs-section">
  <h2 id="d-not-in-flint">Deliberately not in Flint</h2>
  <table class="docs-table">
    <thead>
      <tr><th>M2 variation</th><th>Why not</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>Extended / prominent bar</strong> (128dp, larger
          title)</td><td>Mobile-first native-feel apps don't use them;
          long titles truncate instead (M2's own "don't wrap in a regular
          bar" panel applies).</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Dense / prominent dense</strong> (desktop
          only)</td><td>Density variants contradict the 44px+ touch
          targets everywhere else in Flint.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Images in bars</strong></td><td>Needs the prominent
          height to work and risks illegible text — M2's own "don't"
          panel shows the failure mode.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>iOS translucency</strong></td><td>M2 expresses bar
          separation with elevation; Flint's border is the separation.
          Translucency over scrolling content also fights the opaque
          surface contract. (The web-app need this covered — a bar that
          doesn't draw its own strip — is the
          <a href="#d-transparent">transparent variant</a>.)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Center-aligned titles</strong></td><td>iOS platform
          convention — M2 itself says use the platform's default; the web
          default is left-aligned.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</section>
{% endset %}

